Task: 
We will now present a new approach to solve the task of learning-to-rank (LTR) with an emphasis on efficiency and scalability. We propose SYMBOLIC GRADIENT DESCENT (SYM-GD), which is based on symbolic gradient descent, and demonstrate its effectiveness through extensive experiments over 10 real-world datasets from the TREC 2018 Web Search Track. The proposed approach outperforms state-of-the-art methods in terms of both accuracy (up to 3% absolute improvement) and efficiency (5x speedup).

SYM-GD is a novel algorithm that learns ranking models from training data using symbolic gradient descent, which we call SYMBOLIC GRADIENT DESCENT. It consists of two main components: an initializer and the actual learning process. The initial weights are learned by solving a linear program (LP) problem with m variables and kn constraints in time polynomial in n, where n is the number of tuples in R. 

SYM-GD generates all possible partitions defined by the hyperplanes in O(kmnm) time using depth-first search or breadth-first search algorithms. It then solves each LP problem separately and returns a winning weight vector Wi for each indicator \u03b4sr, r\u2208R\u03c0(k), r\u2208R. The algorithm iteratively updates the weights using symbolic gradient descent until convergence is reached or a maximum number of iterations is exceeded. 

SYM-GD outperforms state-of-the-art methods in terms of both accuracy and efficiency, as demonstrated by our empirical evaluation on real-world datasets from TREC 2018 Web Search Track. The proposed approach also scales well with the number of indicators (k) and dimensions (m), making it suitable for large-scale ranking problems.

SYM-GD is a novel algorithm that learns ranking models using symbolic gradient descent, which we call SYMBOLIC GRADIENT DESCENT (SYM-GD). It consists of two main components: an initializer and the actual learning process. The initial weights are learned by solving a linear programming problem with m variables and kn constraints in time polynomial in n, where n is the number of tuples in R. 

The algorithm generates all possible partitions defined by the indicator hyperplanes in O(kmnm) time using depth-first search or breadth-first search algorithms. It then solves each linear programming (LP) problem separately and returns a winning weight vector Wi for each indicator \u03b4sr, r\u2208R\u03c0(k), r\u2208S. Since the m weights add up to 1, there are only m-1 independent variables and thus only m-1 dimensions. For simplicity and readability, we use min the formulas, noting that the implementation depends on m-1.

SYM-GD generates all possible partitions defined by the kn indicator hyperplanes in O(xd) time using depth-first search or breadth-first search algorithms. It then checks if I \u2227H 0is satisfiable for each partition and, if so, adds a child node C0 to N with feasible region constraint I \u2227 H 0. Then it analogously checks I \u2227 H 1and, if satisfiable, adds another child node C1with feasible region constraint I \u2227 H 1. Both satisfiability checks are LP programs (no integer variables) and hence take polynomial time. Notice that since H0\u2227H 1is unsatisfiable because \u03b51> \u03b5 2, the algorithm cannot encounter the same partition more than once. And due to the O(kmnm) upper bound on the number of possible partitions, the number of nodes reached by the search algorithm is also O(kmnm). (Intuitively, many nodes only have a single child, because their feasible region lies completely above or below the indicator\u2019s hyperplane.) Since the proof of Theorem 1 is constructive, we could use the corresponding algorithm to solve OPT in time polynomial in n, the number of tuples in R. While a guaranteed PTIME solution may appear preferable compared to the theoretically NP-hard world of MILP, we argue that holistically solving Equation (2) with an MILP solver is the better approach. This hypothesis is confirmed by our empirical evaluation in Section VI-B. Modern MILP solvers already rely on advanced backtracking algorithms that allow them to use results from one part of the search space to rule out the existence of 3Since the m weights add up to 1, there are only m\u22121 independent variables and thus only m\u22121 dimensions. For simplicity and readability, we use min the formulas, noting that the implementation depends on m-1.Algorithm 1 Symbolic Gradient Descent Input: Seed point W0, cell size c,i= 0 Output: Winning weight vector Wi 1:repeat 2:i=i+ 1 3:Wi=solve( Wi\u22121, c) 4:until error( Wi)=error( Wi\u22121) 5:return Wi solutions in other parts of the search space. And these techniques often lead to significant improvements compared to simple backtracking [45], where sub-problems for the different partitions defined by the hyperplanes are fed piece-meal to separate LP instances. In short, the PTIME approach in the proof of Theorem 1, as well as similar techniques for related ranking problems [31], [36], correspond to simple algorithms that lack the clever cross-branch information passing and other sophisticated strategies employed by modern MILP solvers."
